{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-306",
              "name": "Unauthenticated access to private member media (photos/videos) via /api/files and /api/video-poster",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated access to private member media (photos/videos) via /api/files and /api/video-poster"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The PocketBase file-serving endpoints `/api/files/<collection>/<recordId>/<filename>` do not enforce authentication for the `posts`, `comments`, `tournament_posts`, `tournament_comments`, `products` and `gallery` collections on this deployment. Any unauthenticated Internet client that knows (or guesses/enumerates) a record id and filename can download the full original media — including 73 MB member-uploaded screen recordings — or fetch server-generated JPEG posters/thumbnails through the custom `/api/video-poster` route, which additionally performs no authentication check of its own."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The PocketBase file-serving endpoints `/api/files/<collection>/<recordId>/<filename>` do not enforce authentication for the `posts`, `comments`, `tournament_posts`, `tournament_comments`, `products` and `gallery` collections on this deployment. Any unauthenticated Internet client that knows (or guesses/enumerates) a record id and filename can download the full original media — including 73 MB member-uploaded screen recordings — or fetch server-generated JPEG posters/thumbnails through the custom `/api/video-poster` route, which additionally performs no authentication check of its own.\n\nFull unauthenticated confidentiality loss for member-uploaded media in the club's private feed and comments, once the media's identifiers are known. The access control that the schema declares (authenticated-only) is not enforced at the file layer, so any party who obtains a record id and stored filename — from a forwarded link, a screenshot, a browser history/cache entry, a referrer, or any other disclosure — can retrieve the original bytes with no session, no token and no rate limit. Verified retrievable anonymously: a 73 MB member screen-recording (via `/api/files`, both full and `?thumb=`) and 42 KB / 54 KB poster frames derived from two different members' private videos (via `/api/video-poster`). The practical constraint is that the identifiers are not anonymously enumerable — see Counterevidence — so this is a broken access-control boundary and an unauthenticated retrieval primitive rather than a directly harvestable corpus.\n\n1. Add an explicit authentication gate to the custom poster route, mirroring its sibling handlers: reject the request with 401 when `c.requestInfo().auth` is empty, before any record lookup or filesystem access.\n2. Verify the effective API rules of the media collections on the live deployment against the exported schema. The `posts` collection is serving files anonymously while its exported `viewRule` is `@request.auth.id != \"\"`; reconcile the two so that PocketBase itself enforces the intended rule at the file endpoint, rather than relying on the SPA to hide URLs.\n3. If anonymous media delivery is in fact intended for some collections, make that explicit in the schema (a documented `viewRule`), rather than leaving the only control in the client.\n4. Where the poster route must resolve records outside the requesting user's permission scope, perform an explicit authorization check on the resolved record (for example, re-fetch it under the request context or compare the record's owning relation against the authenticated user) instead of relying on `$app.findRecordById`, which runs with application privileges.\n5. Apply a rate limit to `/api/files/*` and `/api/video-poster` to make id enumeration impractical.",
                "markdown": "The PocketBase file-serving endpoints `/api/files/<collection>/<recordId>/<filename>` do not enforce authentication for the `posts`, `comments`, `tournament_posts`, `tournament_comments`, `products` and `gallery` collections on this deployment. Any unauthenticated Internet client that knows (or guesses/enumerates) a record id and filename can download the full original media — including 73 MB member-uploaded screen recordings — or fetch server-generated JPEG posters/thumbnails through the custom `/api/video-poster` route, which additionally performs no authentication check of its own.\n\nFull unauthenticated confidentiality loss for member-uploaded media in the club's private feed and comments, once the media's identifiers are known. The access control that the schema declares (authenticated-only) is not enforced at the file layer, so any party who obtains a record id and stored filename — from a forwarded link, a screenshot, a browser history/cache entry, a referrer, or any other disclosure — can retrieve the original bytes with no session, no token and no rate limit. Verified retrievable anonymously: a 73 MB member screen-recording (via `/api/files`, both full and `?thumb=`) and 42 KB / 54 KB poster frames derived from two different members' private videos (via `/api/video-poster`). The practical constraint is that the identifiers are not anonymously enumerable — see Counterevidence — so this is a broken access-control boundary and an unauthenticated retrieval primitive rather than a directly harvestable corpus.\n\n1. Add an explicit authentication gate to the custom poster route, mirroring its sibling handlers: reject the request with 401 when `c.requestInfo().auth` is empty, before any record lookup or filesystem access.\n2. Verify the effective API rules of the media collections on the live deployment against the exported schema. The `posts` collection is serving files anonymously while its exported `viewRule` is `@request.auth.id != \"\"`; reconcile the two so that PocketBase itself enforces the intended rule at the file endpoint, rather than relying on the SPA to hide URLs.\n3. If anonymous media delivery is in fact intended for some collections, make that explicit in the schema (a documented `viewRule`), rather than leaving the only control in the client.\n4. Where the poster route must resolve records outside the requesting user's permission scope, perform an explicit authorization check on the resolved record (for example, re-fetch it under the request context or compare the record's owning relation against the authenticated user) instead of relying on `$app.findRecordById`, which runs with application privileges.\n5. Apply a rate limit to `/api/files/*` and `/api/video-poster` to make id enumeration impractical."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-306",
                  "stride:S",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-862",
              "name": "Broken function-level authorization in /api/bot-notify-training allows any member to send spoofed moderator notifications",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Broken function-level authorization in /api/bot-notify-training allows any member to send spoofed moderator notifications"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The custom PocketBase route `POST /api/bot-notify-training` authenticates the caller but never verifies that they are a moderator, and it accepts the \"acting as moderator\" flag and the list of affected members directly from the request body. Any authenticated low-privilege club member can therefore make the club's MAX bot send moderators a message in the moderator voice, naming an arbitrary other member as the subject of a booking change that never happened."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The custom PocketBase route `POST /api/bot-notify-training` authenticates the caller but never verifies that they are a moderator, and it accepts the \"acting as moderator\" flag and the list of affected members directly from the request body. Any authenticated low-privilege club member can therefore make the club's MAX bot send moderators a message in the moderator voice, naming an arbitrary other member as the subject of a booking change that never happened.\n\nModerators — the club's trusted operators — receive attacker-controlled messages that appear in the application's own moderator voice and are indistinguishable from genuine operational notifications. An attacker can fabricate booking/cancellation events attributed to real members, name any member as the subject, and control the reported booking total. This is a trust and disruption issue rather than a data breach: moderators acting on fabricated booking or cancellation reports may contact or penalise the wrong member, and repeated calls are unbounded, so the channel can be used to flood moderator chats and erode trust in the notification system. It is not a direct path to data disclosure or code execution.\n\n1. Derive the moderator flag on the server rather than trusting the request body: replace the `actorIsModerator` read with a check of the authenticated record's `role` field, exactly as the sibling `bot-notify-*` handlers already do.\n2. Require the moderator role whenever the request targets members other than the caller; a normal member should only be able to trigger notifications about their own booking. Reject other combinations with `403`.\n3. Keep and extend the existing identity binding: validate not only `actorId` but also every id in `userIds` against the caller's permissions for the referenced training.\n4. Validate that the referenced training actually exists and that the claimed booking state matches the stored `booked_users` before generating any message, so the notification reflects stored state rather than client-asserted state.\n5. Apply a per-user rate limit and short-window deduplication to the notification routes so the moderator channel cannot be flooded.",
                "markdown": "The custom PocketBase route `POST /api/bot-notify-training` authenticates the caller but never verifies that they are a moderator, and it accepts the \"acting as moderator\" flag and the list of affected members directly from the request body. Any authenticated low-privilege club member can therefore make the club's MAX bot send moderators a message in the moderator voice, naming an arbitrary other member as the subject of a booking change that never happened.\n\nModerators — the club's trusted operators — receive attacker-controlled messages that appear in the application's own moderator voice and are indistinguishable from genuine operational notifications. An attacker can fabricate booking/cancellation events attributed to real members, name any member as the subject, and control the reported booking total. This is a trust and disruption issue rather than a data breach: moderators acting on fabricated booking or cancellation reports may contact or penalise the wrong member, and repeated calls are unbounded, so the channel can be used to flood moderator chats and erode trust in the notification system. It is not a direct path to data disclosure or code execution.\n\n1. Derive the moderator flag on the server rather than trusting the request body: replace the `actorIsModerator` read with a check of the authenticated record's `role` field, exactly as the sibling `bot-notify-*` handlers already do.\n2. Require the moderator role whenever the request targets members other than the caller; a normal member should only be able to trigger notifications about their own booking. Reject other combinations with `403`.\n3. Keep and extend the existing identity binding: validate not only `actorId` but also every id in `userIds` against the caller's permissions for the referenced training.\n4. Validate that the referenced training actually exists and that the claimed booking state matches the stored `booked_users` before generating any message, so the notification reflects stored state rather than client-asserted state.\n5. Apply a per-user rate limit and short-window deduplication to the notification routes so the moderator channel cannot be flooded."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-862",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-863",
              "name": "Cross-user training history forgery via unrestricted `moderator_kicked_users` / `unbooked_users` writes (PocketBase `trainings` PATCH)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Cross-user training history forgery via unrestricted `moderator_kicked_users` / `unbooked_users` writes (PocketBase `trainings` PATCH)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Any authenticated low-privilege user can write an arbitrary other member's ID into the `moderator_kicked_users` and `unbooked_users` relation fields of any training record via `PATCH /api/collections/trainings/records/{id}`. The server-side guard `applyBookingSideEffects` only validates `booked_users` and `attended_users` (throwing `403 \"Можно записать только себя\"`), so these two fields accept foreign user IDs without any ownership check. The forged entries persist and render in other members' training history as the moderator-attributed badge \"Снят модератором\" (removed by moderator), falsifying club attendance records visible to moderators."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Any authenticated low-privilege user can write an arbitrary other member's ID into the `moderator_kicked_users` and `unbooked_users` relation fields of any training record via `PATCH /api/collections/trainings/records/{id}`. The server-side guard `applyBookingSideEffects` only validates `booked_users` and `attended_users` (throwing `403 \"Можно записать только себя\"`), so these two fields accept foreign user IDs without any ownership check. The forged entries persist and render in other members' training history as the moderator-attributed badge \"Снят модератором\" (removed by moderator), falsifying club attendance records visible to moderators.\n\nAn authenticated ordinary club member can falsify any other member's training history on any training (past, present, or closed), making that member appear to have been removed from a training by a moderator. The forged badge \"Снят модератором\" and the training entry are rendered in that member's profile and in the moderator's member-profile view (`ProfileViewModal` / `ProfilePage`, via `getTrainingStatusForUser`). Because these fields are also consumed by booking eligibility and reporting logic, an attacker can inject spurious \"kicked\"/\"unbooked\" markers into other members' records at will. Every training in the instance is affected, and the write succeeds even on trainings where the attacker is not a participant. This is a cross-user integrity violation of records the business treats as authoritative attendance history.\n\nA second effect of the same root cause is that the attribution mechanism distinguishing a moderator removal from a system removal is also attacker-selectable: `restore_insufficient_users` drives `pb_hooks/trainings_audit.pb.js` to log a removal as a system action rather than a moderator action.\n\nA third effect of the same root cause is that the moderator-only guard on attendance marking can be switched off for a request by supplying `is_cancelled`. The authorization block containing the `attended_users` check is conditioned on transition flags derived from that caller-supplied value, and the exempted branch reaches `adjustAttendanceCountTx`, which writes the server-authoritative `users.attendance_count` with no authorization check. That counter feeds the achievement engine (`client/src/services/achievements.js`, `pb_hooks/achievementslib.js`, and the `profile.achievement.grant` audit path). The counter increment could not be persisted during this assessment because the only reachable training had already ended, which gates that adjustment off — a data-dependent proof gap rather than a control — so it is recorded as a control failure with the persisted effect unproven.\n\nExtend the non-moderator ownership check in the trainings update hook so that every relation field the allow-list exposes to ordinary users is validated against the caller's own ID, not only `booked_users` and `attended_users`.\n\n1. In `applyBookingSideEffects`, additionally compute the added/removed sets for `unbooked_users`, `moderator_kicked_users`, `unpaid_booked_users`, and `restore_insufficient_users`, and require every added or removed identifier to equal the authenticated user's ID for non-moderators. Reject with the existing `ForbiddenError` otherwise.\n2. Alternatively, remove the moderator-semantic fields from the ordinary-user allow-list entirely. `moderator_kicked_users` and `restore_insufficient_users` represent moderator decisions and should not be writable by a plain user under any transition; ordinary users only need to add/remove themselves from `booked_users` (and their own `unbooked_users` entry that accompanies it).\n3. Make the authorization block independent of the cancel/restore transition. The flags `isCancelTransition` and `isRestoreTransition` are derived from the caller-supplied `is_cancelled` value and currently wrap the whole ownership and attendance check, so a caller can select the branch that skips them. Derive any transition handling from the persisted record, or apply the ownership and attendance checks unconditionally and treat the transition only as a side-effect selector.\n4. Treat `users.attendance_count` and the other membership counters as protected state. Updates performed with application privileges inside a transaction bypass the `users` field-level ACL, so any such write should assert the actor's authority explicitly at the point of the write rather than relying on the hook on the `users` collection.\n5. Add a server-side regression test that performs the foreign-ID PATCH described in the PoC and asserts a `403` for each of the four relation fields, including on trainings where the caller is not a participant, plus a case asserting that a non-moderator cannot change attendance through a cancel/restore-shaped request.\n\nBecause this issue is exploitable purely through direct API calls (bypassing the SPA), it cannot be mitigated with client-side changes.",
                "markdown": "Any authenticated low-privilege user can write an arbitrary other member's ID into the `moderator_kicked_users` and `unbooked_users` relation fields of any training record via `PATCH /api/collections/trainings/records/{id}`. The server-side guard `applyBookingSideEffects` only validates `booked_users` and `attended_users` (throwing `403 \"Можно записать только себя\"`), so these two fields accept foreign user IDs without any ownership check. The forged entries persist and render in other members' training history as the moderator-attributed badge \"Снят модератором\" (removed by moderator), falsifying club attendance records visible to moderators.\n\nAn authenticated ordinary club member can falsify any other member's training history on any training (past, present, or closed), making that member appear to have been removed from a training by a moderator. The forged badge \"Снят модератором\" and the training entry are rendered in that member's profile and in the moderator's member-profile view (`ProfileViewModal` / `ProfilePage`, via `getTrainingStatusForUser`). Because these fields are also consumed by booking eligibility and reporting logic, an attacker can inject spurious \"kicked\"/\"unbooked\" markers into other members' records at will. Every training in the instance is affected, and the write succeeds even on trainings where the attacker is not a participant. This is a cross-user integrity violation of records the business treats as authoritative attendance history.\n\nA second effect of the same root cause is that the attribution mechanism distinguishing a moderator removal from a system removal is also attacker-selectable: `restore_insufficient_users` drives `pb_hooks/trainings_audit.pb.js` to log a removal as a system action rather than a moderator action.\n\nA third effect of the same root cause is that the moderator-only guard on attendance marking can be switched off for a request by supplying `is_cancelled`. The authorization block containing the `attended_users` check is conditioned on transition flags derived from that caller-supplied value, and the exempted branch reaches `adjustAttendanceCountTx`, which writes the server-authoritative `users.attendance_count` with no authorization check. That counter feeds the achievement engine (`client/src/services/achievements.js`, `pb_hooks/achievementslib.js`, and the `profile.achievement.grant` audit path). The counter increment could not be persisted during this assessment because the only reachable training had already ended, which gates that adjustment off — a data-dependent proof gap rather than a control — so it is recorded as a control failure with the persisted effect unproven.\n\nExtend the non-moderator ownership check in the trainings update hook so that every relation field the allow-list exposes to ordinary users is validated against the caller's own ID, not only `booked_users` and `attended_users`.\n\n1. In `applyBookingSideEffects`, additionally compute the added/removed sets for `unbooked_users`, `moderator_kicked_users`, `unpaid_booked_users`, and `restore_insufficient_users`, and require every added or removed identifier to equal the authenticated user's ID for non-moderators. Reject with the existing `ForbiddenError` otherwise.\n2. Alternatively, remove the moderator-semantic fields from the ordinary-user allow-list entirely. `moderator_kicked_users` and `restore_insufficient_users` represent moderator decisions and should not be writable by a plain user under any transition; ordinary users only need to add/remove themselves from `booked_users` (and their own `unbooked_users` entry that accompanies it).\n3. Make the authorization block independent of the cancel/restore transition. The flags `isCancelTransition` and `isRestoreTransition` are derived from the caller-supplied `is_cancelled` value and currently wrap the whole ownership and attendance check, so a caller can select the branch that skips them. Derive any transition handling from the persisted record, or apply the ownership and attendance checks unconditionally and treat the transition only as a side-effect selector.\n4. Treat `users.attendance_count` and the other membership counters as protected state. Updates performed with application privileges inside a transaction bypass the `users` field-level ACL, so any such write should assert the actor's authority explicitly at the point of the write rather than relying on the hook on the `users` collection.\n5. Add a server-side regression test that performs the foreign-ID PATCH described in the PoC and asserts a `403` for each of the four relation fields, including on trainings where the caller is not a participant, plus a case asserting that a non-moderator cannot change attendance through a cancel/restore-shaped request.\n\nBecause this issue is exploitable purely through direct API calls (bypassing the SPA), it cannot be mitigated with client-side changes."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-863",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/863.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-798",
              "name": "Production MAX bot token exposed in client-side environment file grants full bot API control and community IPI exfiltration",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Production MAX bot token exposed in client-side environment file grants full bot API control and community IPI exfiltration"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The production MAX messenger **bot token** used by the tennis mini-app is stored in `client/.env` as `MAX_BOT_TOKEN` and, because it is not `.gitignore`d, is committed to the Git repository. The token is **live**: it authenticates against `https://botapi.max.ru` as the real production bot `id420550689204_bot` (\"Тестовый бот\", user id `269693646`). Anyone with repository read access obtains full bot authority — reading the bot's chats and message history, sending messages as the bot to any user, and repointing the bot's webhook to a server they control, which then receives the raw MAX `user_id` of every member who starts the bot."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The production MAX messenger **bot token** used by the tennis mini-app is stored in `client/.env` as `MAX_BOT_TOKEN` and, because it is not `.gitignore`d, is committed to the Git repository. The token is **live**: it authenticates against `https://botapi.max.ru` as the real production bot `id420550689204_bot` (\"Тестовый бот\", user id `269693646`). Anyone with repository read access obtains full bot authority — reading the bot's chats and message history, sending messages as the bot to any user, and repointing the bot's webhook to a server they control, which then receives the raw MAX `user_id` of every member who starts the bot.\n\nAn attacker holding the token gains complete control of the club's official MAX bot for the whole production community. Demonstrated: authenticating as the production bot (`GET /me`), enumerating the bot's chats including members-only group chats with their `owner_id` and invite links (`GET /chats`), and reading the registered webhook ingress URLs with their update types (`GET /subscriptions`). Follow-on consequences available to that position, though not executed during this assessment: sending arbitrary messages to any club member as the trusted official bot (credential/booking phishing), and re-pointing the webhook to an attacker server to harvest the MAX `user_id` of every member who starts the bot — the stable identifier that this application uses as its primary account key. The webhook receiver performs no sender authentication against MAX besides a static secret, so a harvested member list is directly usable for targeted attacks. Because the blast radius is the entire user base rather than a single account, and the secret is a long-term credential with no self-service rotation observable from outside, this is treated as a critical exposure.\n\n1. Revoke the exposed bot token immediately in the MAX partner/developer console and issue a replacement. Treat every secret ever placed in `client/.env` as compromised regardless of Git state.\n2. Remove `MAX_BOT_TOKEN` from `client/.env` entirely. The value is consumed only server-side (`pb_hooks/botlib.js`, `pb_hooks/max-auth.pb.js` read `$os.getenv('MAX_BOT_TOKEN')`), so the frontend has no legitimate use for it.\n3. Supply the token to the backend only, through the process environment or an OS-level secret store, and keep a documented, non-secret placeholder in the example/template files so operators do not copy the real value into the working tree.\n4. Audit the repository history and every tracked configuration file for the credential family; `config/env_app.conf` is tracked and carries a value from the same family. Purge it and add a repository-wide secret-scanning gate so a recurrence fails the build.\n5. If a frontend build could have read the value through a `VITE_`-prefixed variable, rebuild the artefact from a clean environment and invalidate the previous bundle; Vite inlines such variables into the shipped JavaScript.\n6. Store the webhook signing secret (`MAX_BOT_WEBHOOK_SECRET`) separately from the bot token, so that webhook-receiver access and bot-authority access are independent privileges.\n7. Adopt periodic automated rotation of long-lived bot and webhook credentials, with the rotation run from the server environment rather than from a developer checkout.",
                "markdown": "The production MAX messenger **bot token** used by the tennis mini-app is stored in `client/.env` as `MAX_BOT_TOKEN` and, because it is not `.gitignore`d, is committed to the Git repository. The token is **live**: it authenticates against `https://botapi.max.ru` as the real production bot `id420550689204_bot` (\"Тестовый бот\", user id `269693646`). Anyone with repository read access obtains full bot authority — reading the bot's chats and message history, sending messages as the bot to any user, and repointing the bot's webhook to a server they control, which then receives the raw MAX `user_id` of every member who starts the bot.\n\nAn attacker holding the token gains complete control of the club's official MAX bot for the whole production community. Demonstrated: authenticating as the production bot (`GET /me`), enumerating the bot's chats including members-only group chats with their `owner_id` and invite links (`GET /chats`), and reading the registered webhook ingress URLs with their update types (`GET /subscriptions`). Follow-on consequences available to that position, though not executed during this assessment: sending arbitrary messages to any club member as the trusted official bot (credential/booking phishing), and re-pointing the webhook to an attacker server to harvest the MAX `user_id` of every member who starts the bot — the stable identifier that this application uses as its primary account key. The webhook receiver performs no sender authentication against MAX besides a static secret, so a harvested member list is directly usable for targeted attacks. Because the blast radius is the entire user base rather than a single account, and the secret is a long-term credential with no self-service rotation observable from outside, this is treated as a critical exposure.\n\n1. Revoke the exposed bot token immediately in the MAX partner/developer console and issue a replacement. Treat every secret ever placed in `client/.env` as compromised regardless of Git state.\n2. Remove `MAX_BOT_TOKEN` from `client/.env` entirely. The value is consumed only server-side (`pb_hooks/botlib.js`, `pb_hooks/max-auth.pb.js` read `$os.getenv('MAX_BOT_TOKEN')`), so the frontend has no legitimate use for it.\n3. Supply the token to the backend only, through the process environment or an OS-level secret store, and keep a documented, non-secret placeholder in the example/template files so operators do not copy the real value into the working tree.\n4. Audit the repository history and every tracked configuration file for the credential family; `config/env_app.conf` is tracked and carries a value from the same family. Purge it and add a repository-wide secret-scanning gate so a recurrence fails the build.\n5. If a frontend build could have read the value through a `VITE_`-prefixed variable, rebuild the artefact from a clean environment and invalidate the previous bundle; Vite inlines such variables into the shipped JavaScript.\n6. Store the webhook signing secret (`MAX_BOT_WEBHOOK_SECRET`) separately from the bot token, so that webhook-receiver access and bot-authority access are independent privileges.\n7. Adopt periodic automated rotation of long-lived bot and webhook credentials, with the rotation run from the server environment rather than from a developer checkout."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-798",
                  "stride:S",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-942",
              "name": "Wildcard CORS on PocketBase API allows cross-origin reads with a bearer token",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Wildcard CORS on PocketBase API allows cross-origin reads with a bearer token"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The API returns a wildcard Access-Control-Allow-Origin together with authorization advertised as an allowed request header, for any origin."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The API returns a wildcard Access-Control-Allow-Origin together with authorization advertised as an allowed request header, for any origin.\n\nAny JavaScript running in a victim browser can read API responses carrying the victim token and exfiltrate them.\n\nReplace the wildcard origin with an explicit allowlist, stop advertising authorization to unlisted origins, and restrict allowed methods per route.",
                "markdown": "The API returns a wildcard Access-Control-Allow-Origin together with authorization advertised as an allowed request header, for any origin.\n\nAny JavaScript running in a victim browser can read API responses carrying the victim token and exfiltrate them.\n\nReplace the wildcard origin with an explicit allowlist, stop advertising authorization to unlisted origins, and restrict allowed methods per route."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-942",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/942.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-613",
              "name": "Session tokens survive refresh and no server-side logout exists",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Session tokens survive refresh and no server-side logout exists"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Refresh mints a new token while the previous one keeps working, and there is no logout endpoint, so a captured token stays valid for its full lifetime and users cannot end a session."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Refresh mints a new token while the previous one keeps working, and there is no logout endpoint, so a captured token stays valid for its full lifetime and users cannot end a session.\n\nA stolen token remains usable for about seven days with no way for the victim to revoke it, and refreshing does not cut off the old token.\n\nAdd a server-side sign-out endpoint that rotates the record token key, call it from the client sign-out flow, and rotate the token key on each refresh so a refreshed session supersedes the previous credential.",
                "markdown": "Refresh mints a new token while the previous one keeps working, and there is no logout endpoint, so a captured token stays valid for its full lifetime and users cannot end a session.\n\nA stolen token remains usable for about seven days with no way for the victim to revoke it, and refreshing does not cut off the old token.\n\nAdd a server-side sign-out endpoint that rotates the record token key, call it from the client sign-out flow, and rotate the token key on each refresh so a refreshed session supersedes the previous credential."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-613",
                  "stride:S"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/613.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-639",
              "name": "Broken object-level authorization on notifications collection allows any user to reassign someone else's notification (forged in-app notifications)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Broken object-level authorization on notifications collection allows any user to reassign someone else's notification (forged in-app notifications)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The PocketBase `notifications` collection accepts an UPDATE from any authenticated user that rewrites the `recipient` field to a different user's ID. A plain (non-moderator) account can therefore take a notification it owns, replace its text with arbitrary content, and re-address it to any other user — including moderators. Because the client lists notifications by `recipient = <my id>`, the forged record is displayed inside the victim's notification feed, impersonating official in-app messages."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The PocketBase `notifications` collection accepts an UPDATE from any authenticated user that rewrites the `recipient` field to a different user's ID. A plain (non-moderator) account can therefore take a notification it owns, replace its text with arbitrary content, and re-address it to any other user — including moderators. Because the client lists notifications by `recipient = <my id>`, the forged record is displayed inside the victim's notification feed, impersonating official in-app messages.\n\nAn authenticated low-privilege user can inject arbitrary, attacker-controlled text into another user's in-app notification feed. Notifications are a trusted, branded channel in this application (membership expiry warnings, training reminders, broadcast messages) and the card renders the record's own `title`/`body` with a click action, so a forged card is indistinguishable from an official one. This supports targeted phishing/social engineering (e.g. \"оплатите абонемент по ссылке\", \"ваша запись отменена\") and can be aimed at moderators, who hold administrative functions. It also permits one-off corruption of a victim's notification state and, in combination with the notification deep-link handler, attacker-chosen navigation targets for social notifications (`meta.collection` / `meta.postId`). The write is unauthenticated-to-authorization-bypass: no moderator role and no ownership of the target user is required.\n\n1. Tighten the `notifications` update API rule so that a record can only be updated by the authenticated user it belongs to and so that the `recipient` field cannot change: for example require `recipient = @request.auth.id` on the update rule and keep the \"current recipient\" equality requirement, so that any update whose new `recipient` differs from the stored one is rejected.\n2. Serve the field value through a server-side guard as well as the rule: add an `onRecordUpdateRequest` hook on `notifications` that throws a `ForbiddenError` when `e.record.get('recipient') !== e.record.original().get('recipient')` for non-superuser callers, mirroring the existing `pb_hooks/users_access_guard.pb.js` pattern already used for the `users` collection.\n3. Restrict client-side creation of notifications. Privileged notification types (membership, training, broadcast) should be produced server-side by the existing hooks rather than by `client/src/services/notifications.js:123` (`maybeNotifySessionsLeft`), which lets any caller choose a `recipient`. If client-side creation must remain, bind `recipient` to `@request.auth.id` in the create rule.\n4. Treat notification `title`/`body`/`badge_text` as trusted branding: keep them immutable to non-superusers (consider excluding them from the update rule, or restricting updates to the `is_read` field) so the feed cannot be used as a phishing surface.\n5. Re-run authorization checks across the remaining collections with the same matrix (create/update/delete × own/foreign/nonexistent) — comments, gallery and tournament comments, posts, tournament_posts, trainings, products, achievements and the audit/log collections — since the notifications update rule shows the rules were written per-collection and at least one was left loose.",
                "markdown": "The PocketBase `notifications` collection accepts an UPDATE from any authenticated user that rewrites the `recipient` field to a different user's ID. A plain (non-moderator) account can therefore take a notification it owns, replace its text with arbitrary content, and re-address it to any other user — including moderators. Because the client lists notifications by `recipient = <my id>`, the forged record is displayed inside the victim's notification feed, impersonating official in-app messages.\n\nAn authenticated low-privilege user can inject arbitrary, attacker-controlled text into another user's in-app notification feed. Notifications are a trusted, branded channel in this application (membership expiry warnings, training reminders, broadcast messages) and the card renders the record's own `title`/`body` with a click action, so a forged card is indistinguishable from an official one. This supports targeted phishing/social engineering (e.g. \"оплатите абонемент по ссылке\", \"ваша запись отменена\") and can be aimed at moderators, who hold administrative functions. It also permits one-off corruption of a victim's notification state and, in combination with the notification deep-link handler, attacker-chosen navigation targets for social notifications (`meta.collection` / `meta.postId`). The write is unauthenticated-to-authorization-bypass: no moderator role and no ownership of the target user is required.\n\n1. Tighten the `notifications` update API rule so that a record can only be updated by the authenticated user it belongs to and so that the `recipient` field cannot change: for example require `recipient = @request.auth.id` on the update rule and keep the \"current recipient\" equality requirement, so that any update whose new `recipient` differs from the stored one is rejected.\n2. Serve the field value through a server-side guard as well as the rule: add an `onRecordUpdateRequest` hook on `notifications` that throws a `ForbiddenError` when `e.record.get('recipient') !== e.record.original().get('recipient')` for non-superuser callers, mirroring the existing `pb_hooks/users_access_guard.pb.js` pattern already used for the `users` collection.\n3. Restrict client-side creation of notifications. Privileged notification types (membership, training, broadcast) should be produced server-side by the existing hooks rather than by `client/src/services/notifications.js:123` (`maybeNotifySessionsLeft`), which lets any caller choose a `recipient`. If client-side creation must remain, bind `recipient` to `@request.auth.id` in the create rule.\n4. Treat notification `title`/`body`/`badge_text` as trusted branding: keep them immutable to non-superusers (consider excluding them from the update rule, or restricting updates to the `is_read` field) so the feed cannot be used as a phishing surface.\n5. Re-run authorization checks across the remaining collections with the same matrix (create/update/delete × own/foreign/nonexistent) — comments, gallery and tournament comments, posts, tournament_posts, trainings, products, achievements and the audit/log collections — since the notifications update rule shows the rules were written per-collection and at least one was left loose."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-602",
              "name": "Custom content-view route validation bypassed by direct collection write, allowing unbounded self-attributed metric rows",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Custom content-view route validation bypassed by direct collection write, allowing unbounded self-attributed metric rows"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The application provides a dedicated endpoint, `POST /api/content-view`, that validates view-tracking input before storing it. The underlying `content_views` collection, however, remains directly writable through the generic PocketBase collection API with only a self-scoped rule, so an authenticated member can create view rows carrying `object_type` / `object_id` / `source` values that the application's own validator rejects. The rows cannot be read back or deleted by any non-superuser, so they accumulate permanently in the table that the moderator statistics dashboard aggregates."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The application provides a dedicated endpoint, `POST /api/content-view`, that validates view-tracking input before storing it. The underlying `content_views` collection, however, remains directly writable through the generic PocketBase collection API with only a self-scoped rule, so an authenticated member can create view rows carrying `object_type` / `object_id` / `source` values that the application's own validator rejects. The rows cannot be read back or deleted by any non-superuser, so they accumulate permanently in the table that the moderator statistics dashboard aggregates.\n\nA member can write arbitrary, permanently-retained rows into the content-view metrics table. The application's validation — which restricts `object_type` to `post` or `tournament_post`, requires a non-empty `object_id`, and restricts `source` to `viewport` or `modal` — is bypassed. Because `content_views` has no delete rule for non-superusers, the rows cannot be cleaned up by their author, so the distortion is durable. The direct impact is capped: the statistics consumers are moderator-only and the reach report re-resolves each object server-side before presenting it, so a fabricated `object_id` does not inject a spurious entry into the displayed top-content list. The practical effect is inflated or meaningless view-metric data and unbounded table growth in an analytics collection.\n\nMake the data store enforce the same constraints the application route enforces.\n\n1. Tighten the `content_views` collection rules so the generic record endpoint cannot be used as an alternate write path: restrict creation to server-side code (set the create rule to a value no client can satisfy and have the `POST /api/content-view` handler perform the write with application privileges), or add a collection-level `onRecordCreateRequest` hook that applies the same `object_type` / `object_id` / `source` validation as `createContentView`.\n2. Apply the validation in one place and reuse it: have the collection hook call the same logic used by the route, so the two paths cannot drift apart again.\n3. Add a delete rule (or a maintenance job) so that erroneous or abusive rows can be removed; currently no non-superuser can delete a row, and there is no visible retention policy for this table.\n4. Add rate limiting or server-side de-duplication (for example, one row per user/object/time-window) to bound how many rows a single account can create, on both the route and the collection endpoint.\n5. Purge the test rows left in the table: rows with `object_id` values prefixed `zqath3` were created during this assessment and could not be deleted through the API.",
                "markdown": "The application provides a dedicated endpoint, `POST /api/content-view`, that validates view-tracking input before storing it. The underlying `content_views` collection, however, remains directly writable through the generic PocketBase collection API with only a self-scoped rule, so an authenticated member can create view rows carrying `object_type` / `object_id` / `source` values that the application's own validator rejects. The rows cannot be read back or deleted by any non-superuser, so they accumulate permanently in the table that the moderator statistics dashboard aggregates.\n\nA member can write arbitrary, permanently-retained rows into the content-view metrics table. The application's validation — which restricts `object_type` to `post` or `tournament_post`, requires a non-empty `object_id`, and restricts `source` to `viewport` or `modal` — is bypassed. Because `content_views` has no delete rule for non-superusers, the rows cannot be cleaned up by their author, so the distortion is durable. The direct impact is capped: the statistics consumers are moderator-only and the reach report re-resolves each object server-side before presenting it, so a fabricated `object_id` does not inject a spurious entry into the displayed top-content list. The practical effect is inflated or meaningless view-metric data and unbounded table growth in an analytics collection.\n\nMake the data store enforce the same constraints the application route enforces.\n\n1. Tighten the `content_views` collection rules so the generic record endpoint cannot be used as an alternate write path: restrict creation to server-side code (set the create rule to a value no client can satisfy and have the `POST /api/content-view` handler perform the write with application privileges), or add a collection-level `onRecordCreateRequest` hook that applies the same `object_type` / `object_id` / `source` validation as `createContentView`.\n2. Apply the validation in one place and reuse it: have the collection hook call the same logic used by the route, so the two paths cannot drift apart again.\n3. Add a delete rule (or a maintenance job) so that erroneous or abusive rows can be removed; currently no non-superuser can delete a row, and there is no visible retention policy for this table.\n4. Add rate limiting or server-side de-duplication (for example, one row per user/object/time-window) to bound how many rows a single account can create, on both the route and the collection endpoint.\n5. Purge the test rows left in the table: rows with `object_id` values prefixed `zqath3` were created during this assessment and could not be deleted through the API."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-602",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/602.html"
            },
