      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-306",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unauthenticated access to private member media (photos/videos) via /api/files and /api/video-poster\n\nThe PocketBase file-serving endpoints `/api/files/<collection>/<recordId>/<filename>` do not enforce authentication for the `posts`, `comments`, `tournament_posts`, `tournament_comments`, `products` and `gallery` collections on this deployment. Any unauthenticated Internet client that knows (or guesses/enumerates) a record id and filename can download the full original media — including 73 MB member-uploaded screen recordings — or fetch server-generated JPEG posters/thumbnails through the custom `/api/video-poster` route, which additionally performs no authentication check of its own."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "pb_hooks/video_poster.pb.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 124,
                  "endLine": 133,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "routerAdd('GET', '/api/video-poster', (c) => {\n  try {\n    var poster = require(__hooks + '/video_poster_lib.js');\n    var query = c.requestInfo().query || {};\n    var collection = query.collection || '';\n    var recordId = query.record || query.recordId || '';\n    var filename = query.file || query.filename || '';\n    var thumbSize = poster.parseThumbSize(query.thumb || '');\n\n    if (!collection || !recordId || !filename) {"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Add the missing authentication gate to the poster route (siblings all check info.auth)"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/files/{collection}/{recordId}/{filename}",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "fixes": [
            {
              "artifactChanges": [
                {
                  "artifactLocation": {
                    "uri": "pb_hooks/video_poster.pb.js"
                  },
                  "replacements": [
                    {
                      "deletedRegion": {
                        "startLine": 124,
                        "endLine": 133
                      },
                      "insertedContent": {
                        "text": "routerAdd('GET', '/api/video-poster', (c) => {\n  try {\n    var poster = require(__hooks + '/video_poster_lib.js');\n    var info = c.requestInfo();\n    if (!info.auth) {\n      return c.json(401, { error: 'Unauthorized' });\n    }\n    var query = info.query || {};\n    var collection = query.collection || '';\n    var recordId = query.record || query.recordId || '';\n    var filename = query.file || query.filename || '';\n    var thumbSize = poster.parseThumbSize(query.thumb || '');\n\n    if (!collection || !recordId || !filename) {"
                      }
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "description": {
                "text": "1. Add an explicit authentication gate to the custom poster route, mirroring its sibling handlers: reject the request with 401 when `c.requestInfo().auth` is empty, before any record lookup or filesystem access.\n2. Verify the effective API rules of the media collections on the live deployment against the exported schema. The `posts` collection is serving files anonymously while its exported `viewRule` is `@request.auth.id != \"\"`; reconcile the two so that PocketBase itself enforces the intended rule at the file endpoint, rather than relying on the SPA to hide URLs.\n3. If anonymous media delivery is in fact intended for some collections, make that explicit in the schema (a documented `viewRule`), rather than leaving the only control in the client.\n4. Where the poster route must resolve records outside the requesting user's permission scope, perform an explicit authorization check on the resolved record (for example, re-fetch it under the request context or compare the record's owning relation against the authenticated user) instead of relying on `$app.findRecordById`, which runs with application privileges.\n5. Apply a rate limit to `/api/files/*` and `/api/video-poster` to make id enumeration impractical.",
                "markdown": "1. Add an explicit authentication gate to the custom poster route, mirroring its sibling handlers: reject the request with 401 when `c.requestInfo().auth` is empty, before any record lookup or filesystem access.\n2. Verify the effective API rules of the media collections on the live deployment against the exported schema. The `posts` collection is serving files anonymously while its exported `viewRule` is `@request.auth.id != \"\"`; reconcile the two so that PocketBase itself enforces the intended rule at the file endpoint, rather than relying on the SPA to hide URLs.\n3. If anonymous media delivery is in fact intended for some collections, make that explicit in the schema (a documented `viewRule`), rather than leaving the only control in the client.\n4. Where the poster route must resolve records outside the requesting user's permission scope, perform an explicit authorization check on the resolved record (for example, re-fetch it under the request context or compare the record's owning relation against the authenticated user) instead of relying on `$app.findRecordById`, which runs with application privileges.\n5. Apply a rate limit to `/api/files/*` and `/api/video-poster` to make id enumeration impractical."
              }
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "467f9c0da9bb5ed9f226647402b7b150ae47ff50fc5e93639009f4081e4717de"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "ed3d7fb6f92558c1391a2f7d42b58da9a3c492a78242b1219eb983cadaa2b2ce",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-12 15:13:06 UTC",
              "target": "https://app.milenkih-team.ru",
              "endpoint": "/api/files/{collection}/{recordId}/{filename}",
              "method": "GET",
              "cwe": "CWE-306",
              "impact": "Full unauthenticated confidentiality loss for member-uploaded media in the club's private feed and comments, once the media's identifiers are known. The access control that the schema declares (authenticated-only) is not enforced at the file layer, so any party who obtains a record id and stored filename — from a forwarded link, a screenshot, a browser history/cache entry, a referrer, or any other disclosure — can retrieve the original bytes with no session, no token and no rate limit. Verified retrievable anonymously: a 73 MB member screen-recording (via `/api/files`, both full and `?thumb=`) and 42 KB / 54 KB poster frames derived from two different members' private videos (via `/api/video-poster`). The practical constraint is that the identifiers are not anonymously enumerable — see Counterevidence — so this is a broken access-control boundary and an unauthenticated retrieval primitive rather than a directly harvestable corpus.",
              "technical_analysis": "PocketBase serves stored files at `/api/files/{collection}/{recordId}/{filename}` and applies the collection's **viewRule** (and, for protected collections, `@request.auth.id != \"\"`) before returning bytes. The exported schema declares `viewRule: '@request.auth.id != \"\"'` for `posts`, `comments`, `gallery`, `tournament_posts`, `tournament_comments` and `products`, so anonymous requests should be rejected with 404.\n\nObserved behaviour contradicts the schema for the `posts` collection: an anonymous request returns real bytes.\n\nThe custom poster route compounds this. `pb_hooks/video_poster.pb.js` is the only `routerAdd` handler in the project with no authentication gate — every sibling (`/api/yadisk-preview`, `/api/notify-comment-reply`, `/api/content-view`, `/api/bot-notify-*`, `/api/users-claim-*`, `/api/admin-*`) starts with `if (!info.auth) return c.json(401, ...)`, while the poster route goes straight from `c.requestInfo().query` to the filesystem:\n\n```javascript title=pb_hooks/video_poster.pb.js startLineNumber=124 endLineNumber=155\nrouterAdd('GET', '/api/video-poster', (c) => {\n  try {\n    var poster = require(__hooks + '/video_poster_lib.js');\n    var query = c.requestInfo().query || {};\n    var collection = query.collection || '';\n    var recordId = query.record || query.recordId || '';\n    var filename = query.file || query.filename || '';\n    var thumbSize = poster.parseThumbSize(query.thumb || '');\n\n    if (!collection || !recordId || !filename) {\n      return c.json(400, { error: 'missing_params' });\n    }\n\n    if (!poster.getMediaTarget(collection)) {\n      return c.json(404, { error: 'not_found' });\n    }\n\n    var record;\n    try {\n      record = $app.findRecordById(collection, recordId);\n    } catch (_) {\n      return c.json(404, { error: 'not_found' });\n    }\n\n    var resolved = poster.resolvePosterFile(record, filename, thumbSize);\n    if (resolved.error) {\n      return c.json(resolved.status || 400, { error: resolved.error });\n    }\n\n    var bytes = $os.readFile(resolved.path);\n```\n\nThe route resolves the record with `$app.findRecordById` (which bypasses request-scoped rules entirely, because it executes as the application rather than as the requesting user) and streams the file from disk with `$os.readFile`. It returns `Content-Type: image/jpeg` for any file whose name matches `\\.(mp4|webm|mov|m4v|mkv|avi)$`, so a request naming a video produces a poster/thumbnail derived from that member's private video.\n\nFilename traversal is *not* exploitable here — `resolvePosterFile` requires the requested filename to be a literal member of the record's media array (`names.indexOf(videoFilename) < 0` → 404) and `record.baseFilesPath()` returns the server-stored sanitized name — but authorization is absent.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Add an explicit authentication gate to the custom poster route, mirroring its sibling handlers: reject the request with 401 when `c.requestInfo().auth` is empty, before any record lookup or filesystem access.\n2. Verify the effective API rules of the media collections on the live deployment against the exported schema. The `posts` collection is serving files anonymously while its exported `viewRule` is `@request.auth.id != \"\"`; reconcile the two so that PocketBase itself enforces the intended rule at the file endpoint, rather than relying on the SPA to hide URLs.\n3. If anonymous media delivery is in fact intended for some collections, make that explicit in the schema (a documented `viewRule`), rather than leaving the only control in the client.\n4. Where the poster route must resolve records outside the requesting user's permission scope, perform an explicit authorization check on the resolved record (for example, re-fetch it under the request context or compare the record's owning relation against the authenticated user) instead of relying on `$app.findRecordById`, which runs with application privileges.\n5. Apply a rate limit to `/api/files/*` and `/api/video-poster` to make id enumeration impractical.",
              "counterevidence": "Actively tested the alternative explanations and the constraints. (a) *\"The 200 was a proxy artefact\"* — ruled out by repeating with fresh connections and a `Range` header: the response carries a genuine `206 Partial Content`, `content-range: bytes 0-99/73405665` and real MPEG-4 box bytes. (b) *\"nginx/CDN is caching an object fetched by an authenticated user\"* — the poster route returns a freshly generated `image/jpeg` with `Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, immutable` and `Vary: Origin`; a cached static object would not produce a JPEG for a record whose poster is created lazily by `ffmpeg`. (c) *\"access control is simply not loaded\"* — no: the same host returns `404` for unknown record ids and `200` with populated rows for authenticated calls, so rules are evaluated, just not for media. (d) *\"nginx front-loads the auth check\"* — no: sibling custom routes such as `/api/yadisk-preview` return `401 {\"error\":\"Unauthorized\"}` anonymously, so nginx forwards custom routes untouched and the omission is in the handler. (e) **Material constraint found:** unknown record ids return `404` and `GET /api/collections/posts/records` without a token returns `200` with `{\"items\":[],\"totalItems\":0}`, so ids are *not* anonymously enumerable; exploitation requires a known `(recordId, filename)` pair. That is why the rating is high rather than critical, and it is stated in the impact. (f) A benign reading — that anonymous media delivery is intended for a public club feed — is undermined by the repository's own schema export declaring `viewRule: '@request.auth.id != \"\"'` for these collections, and by the fact that the poster route is the only custom handler in the project without an auth check.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "confidence_rationale": "Live unauthenticated retrieval of member media was reproduced repeatedly with byte-level evidence (206 Partial Content, MPEG-4 `ftyp` box and `Lavc60.31.102` encoder tag, genuine JPEG poster frames), and the missing authentication gate in `pb_hooks/video_poster.pb.js` is confirmed by reading the source against its siblings. The one gap below certainty is scope: record ids could not be enumerated anonymously, so the finding is rated for unauthorized retrieval of media whose identifiers are known, not for mass harvesting.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that record ids and stored filenames for private media are systematically disclosed or enumerable by an unauthenticated party would raise this to critical, because the corpus would then be directly harvestable; conversely, evidence that anonymous media delivery is a deliberate, documented product decision for these collections would lower it to medium.",
              "fix_verification": "**Gate 1 — applicability.** The `fix_before` block is a character-for-character copy of `pb_hooks/video_poster.pb.js` lines 124-133 (verified by reading the file directly). `start_line`/`end_line` cover exactly those lines. The patch introduces no unrelated changes. Syntax: the edit only adds two statements and rewrites `c.requestInfo()` into a local `info` variable that the surrounding code already uses; the rest of the handler (`info.query`, unchanged below the block) continues to compile.\n\n**Gate 2 — security closure.** Re-traced the source→sink path through the patched code: `routerAdd('GET','/api/video-poster')` → new `if (!info.auth) return c.json(401,...)` → `query = info.query` → `$app.findRecordById` → `poster.resolvePosterFile` → `$os.readFile`. The only path that reaches `resolvePosterFile` now requires `info.auth` to be truthy, so an anonymous request terminates at the new 401 before any record lookup or disk read. Same for the `$os.cmd(ffmpeg...)` path invoked inside `resolvePosterFile` via `ensurePosterForVideo`. Empirically, the pre-patch anonymous request returned `200 image/jpeg` with 54,339 bytes; the patched request must return `401` with a 25-byte JSON body. **Not executed against the live deployment** — see Gate 4.\n\n**Gate 3 — bypass review.** Checked the alternate input classes and sibling paths: (a) *other HTTP verbs* — only `GET` is registered for `/api/video-poster`; a `POST` would hit the SPA fallback or a 404, not the handler; (b) *case/encoded path variants* (`/api/video-poster/`, `%2F`, trailing slash) — PocketBase's router matches on the registered pattern and forwards to the same handler, so the gate applies identically; (c) *equivalent sinks* — the same data is reachable through `/api/files/...`, which is a **different** control (PocketBase's collection `viewRule`) and is explicitly called out as a follow-up rather than silently closed by this patch; (d) *alternate malicious inputs* — filename traversal (`../../../../etc/passwd.mp4`) was tested live and returned `404`, and the gate does not affect that behaviour; (e) *sibling routes* — `/api/bot-notify-training-status`, `-edit`, `-cancelled`, `-restored`, `-create` and `/api/yadisk-preview` were each read and already enforce a role or auth check, so they are unaffected.\n\n**Gate 4 — preserved behaviour.** The legitimate caller is the SPA. `client/src` reaches the poster endpoint through its media helper with the PocketBase auth token attached (`pb.authStore.token`), so authenticated users continue to receive posters unchanged; only anonymous callers are rejected. Error semantics are preserved: the handler's existing `{ error: ... }` JSON shape is reused, and the `400`/`404`/`500` branches below the new gate are untouched. **Gap:** this was verified by reading the client caller, not by running the SPA end-to-end.\n\n**Gate 5 — repository checks.** `pb_hooks/*.js` are PocketBase JSVM hook scripts with no package manifest, test runner, linter configuration or type-check step in this repository (`pb_hooks/` contains only runtime scripts; the `@ts-check` directives live in `client/src`). The narrowest available check is therefore a parse of the edited file. **Gap:** no repository-provided command exists to execute; a `node --check` parse was not run because the edited file references PocketBase globals (`routerAdd`, `$app`, `$os`) that are only defined by the JSVM at runtime, and Node's parser would accept the file regardless of those globals.\n\n**Explicit gaps.** Gate 2 was reasoned through the patched source and the pre-patch live behaviour, but the patched handler was not deployed and re-requested — that is the single check a reviewer should run first. Gate 4's SPA flow and Gate 5's absence of tooling are likewise recorded rather than executed.\n\n**Narrowest complete change.** The patch adds exactly the invariant the route is missing (an authenticated principal) at the single point where every input is consumed, reusing the pattern already used by the neighbouring handlers, with no refactoring of the query parsing, record resolution or file streaming.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Without any `Authorization` header, request a member's uploaded video through the standard PocketBase file endpoint, using a `Range` header to keep the response small.\n2. Observe `206 Partial Content` and real MPEG-4 bytes (`ftyp`/`moov` box magic, `Lavc60.31.102` encoder string) — the private media is returned to an anonymous client.\n3. Without any `Authorization` header, request the same media through the custom poster route `/api/video-poster` and observe `200 OK`, `Content-Type: image/jpeg`, `Content-Length: 54339` — a JPEG poster frame rendered from the private video.\n4. Repeat (1) with the `thumb=100x100` parameter and observe `206 Partial Content`, confirming PocketBase's own thumbnailer also serves anonymous clients.\n5. For contrast, request a non-existent record id (`aaaaaaaaaaaaaaa`) and observe `404`, confirming the endpoint is reachable and simply enforcing no authentication.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-862",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Broken function-level authorization in /api/bot-notify-training allows any member to send spoofed moderator notifications\n\nThe custom PocketBase route `POST /api/bot-notify-training` authenticates the caller but never verifies that they are a moderator, and it accepts the \"acting as moderator\" flag and the list of affected members directly from the request body. Any authenticated low-privilege club member can therefore make the club's MAX bot send moderators a message in the moderator voice, naming an arbitrary other member as the subject of a booking change that never happened."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/bot-notify-training",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "819b33dc5053e5b25b4b0cbbeecc1b4dbd61c5719c09ca8f7ed5d7a35805e31a"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "d7c920010c9f23b94628a4bff3313ffa3e22ab46a013b2f4cf84bc58c51bf50c",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0002",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-12 15:13:51 UTC",
              "target": "https://app.milenkih-team.ru",
              "endpoint": "/api/bot-notify-training",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-862",
              "impact": "Moderators — the club's trusted operators — receive attacker-controlled messages that appear in the application's own moderator voice and are indistinguishable from genuine operational notifications. An attacker can fabricate booking/cancellation events attributed to real members, name any member as the subject, and control the reported booking total. This is a trust and disruption issue rather than a data breach: moderators acting on fabricated booking or cancellation reports may contact or penalise the wrong member, and repeated calls are unbounded, so the channel can be used to flood moderator chats and erode trust in the notification system. It is not a direct path to data disclosure or code execution.",
              "technical_analysis": "`pb_hooks/bot_notifications.pb.js` registers six bot-notification routes. Five of them (`/api/bot-notify-training-status`, `-edit`, `-cancelled`, `-restored`, `-create`) enforce the moderator role immediately after the authentication check:\n\n```javascript\nif (auth.getString('role') !== 'moderator') {\n  return c.json(403, { error: 'Forbidden' });\n}\n```\n\n`POST /api/bot-notify-training` omits that check. It authenticates (`if (!auth) 401`), then reads the behaviour-determining values straight out of the request body:\n\n```javascript\nconst actorId = body.actorId;\nconst actorIsModerator = !!body.actorIsModerator;\nconst totalBookedCount = body.totalBookedCount;\n```\n\nThe only identity binding applied is `if (actorId !== auth.id) return c.json(403, ...)`. That prevents attributing the message to a *third party*, but it does not restrict what the caller may claim about themselves, which members the message names, or whether the action is rendered with the moderator wording.\n\nThe message text is then selected from the attacker-controlled flag (`const isModAction = actorIsModerator && (userIds.length > 1 || userIds[0] !== actorId);`) and delivered with application privileges via `bot.notifyModerators(text)`. `notifyModerators` (in `pb_hooks/botlib.js`) selects recipients with `$app.findRecordsByFilter('users', 'role = \"moderator\" && max_id != \"\"', ...)` — bypassing request-scoped rules — and calls `sendBotMessage`, which issues a real `POST https://botapi.max.ru/messages?user_id=<maxId>` carrying the `MAX_BOT_TOKEN`. The effect therefore crosses the trust boundary from an ordinary member into moderator MAX chats, in the club's own bot voice.\n\nThe SPA supplies the correct value (`actorIsModerator: isModerator()` in `client/src/services/trainings.js`), which is why the gap is invisible in normal use: it only manifests when the request is issued directly rather than through the UI.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Derive the moderator flag on the server rather than trusting the request body: replace the `actorIsModerator` read with a check of the authenticated record's `role` field, exactly as the sibling `bot-notify-*` handlers already do.\n2. Require the moderator role whenever the request targets members other than the caller; a normal member should only be able to trigger notifications about their own booking. Reject other combinations with `403`.\n3. Keep and extend the existing identity binding: validate not only `actorId` but also every id in `userIds` against the caller's permissions for the referenced training.\n4. Validate that the referenced training actually exists and that the claimed booking state matches the stored `booked_users` before generating any message, so the notification reflects stored state rather than client-asserted state.\n5. Apply a per-user rate limit and short-window deduplication to the notification routes so the moderator channel cannot be flooded.",
              "counterevidence": "Actively hunted for a control that would neutralise this. (a) *\"Maybe the body flag is ignored server-side\"* — no: the handler reads `const actorIsModerator = !!body.actorIsModerator;` and uses it directly in `isModAction`, with no comparison against `auth`. (b) *\"Maybe a client-side guard is sufficient\"* — the SPA sends `actorIsModerator: isModerator()`, but the server is the trust boundary and the request is a plain `POST` to `/api/bot-notify-training` that any holder of a token can replay; a client-side boolean is not a control. (c) *\"Maybe only the actor can be named\"* — `actorId` is checked against `auth.id`, but `userIds` is not, so the *victim* named in the text is attacker-chosen and wholly unrelated to the actor. (d) *\"Maybe the message is an in-app notification the team ignores\"* — no, `bot.notifyModerators` enumerates `role = \"moderator\" && max_id != \"\"` and calls `sendBotMessage`, which POSTs to `https://botapi.max.ru/messages?user_id=...`; this is a real MAX messenger message. (e) *\"Maybe authorization is enforced upstream by nginx\"* — no: nginx forwards `/api/*` custom routes unchanged, as shown by sibling routes returning their own JSON `401`/`403` bodies. (f) The remaining broadcast handlers (`-create`, `-cancelled`, `-restored`, `-status`, `-edit`) **do** carry `auth.getString('role') !== 'moderator'` checks, which confirms this handler's missing check is an oversight rather than intentional design, and that the developers knew the correct pattern. (g) The one genuine constraint found is that `actorId` must equal the caller's own id, so the attacker cannot name an arbitrary *third party* as the moderator — they can only impersonate themselves in a moderator voice. That limits the finding to high rather than critical.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Demonstrating that a member can additionally attribute the fabricated action to a named third-party moderator (rather than only to themselves in moderator voice) would raise this to critical, because moderators would then be unable to distinguish impersonated actions from genuine ones at all; conversely, evidence that the moderator chat channel is not operationally relied upon would lower it to medium.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Obtain a low-privilege member session (any club member account); confirm its record has `role = \"user\"`.\n2. Send an authenticated `POST /api/bot-notify-training` whose body sets `actorIsModerator` to `true` and whose `userIds` names a *different* member than the caller, with `actorId` set to the caller's own id.\n3. Observe `HTTP 200` and `{\"success\":true}` — the route performs no role check and accepts the caller-supplied moderator flag.\n4. The handler renders the message using the moderator wording (`Модератор <actorName> снял <victim> с тренировки ...`) and passes it to `bot.notifyModerators`, which sends a MAX messenger message to every user with `role = \"moderator\"`.\n5. Re-send the same request repeatedly to demonstrate the absence of any rate limit or deduplication; each call delivers another message.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-863",
          "ruleIndex": 2,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Cross-user training history forgery via unrestricted `moderator_kicked_users` / `unbooked_users` writes (PocketBase `trainings` PATCH)\n\nAny authenticated low-privilege user can write an arbitrary other member's ID into the `moderator_kicked_users` and `unbooked_users` relation fields of any training record via `PATCH /api/collections/trainings/records/{id}`. The server-side guard `applyBookingSideEffects` only validates `booked_users` and `attended_users` (throwing `403 \"Можно записать только себя\"`), so these two fields accept foreign user IDs without any ownership check. The forged entries persist and render in other members' training history as the moderator-attributed badge \"Снят модератором\" (removed by moderator), falsifying club attendance records visible to moderators."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "pb_hooks/trainingslib.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 303,
                  "endLine": 310,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "/** Поля, которые обычный user может менять (запись/снятие себя). */\nvar TRAINING_USER_EDITABLE = {\n  booked_users: true,\n  unbooked_users: true,\n  unpaid_booked_users: true,\n  moderator_kicked_users: true,\n  restore_insufficient_users: true\n};"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Allow-list that exposes moderator-semantic fields to ordinary users"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "pb_hooks/trainingslib.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 394,
                  "endLine": 412,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "// --- IDOR (C-6): обычный пользователь — только себя; посещаемость — только модератор ---\n  if (!isCancelTransition && !isRestoreTransition) {\n    if (added.length && !isModerator) {\n      for (i = 0; i < added.length; i++) {\n        if (String(added[i]) !== authId) {\n          throw new ForbiddenError('Можно записать только себя');\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    if (removed.length && !isModerator) {\n      for (i = 0; i < removed.length; i++) {\n        if (String(removed[i]) !== authId) {\n          throw new ForbiddenError('Можно снять с записи только себя');\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    if ((attendedAdded.length || attendedRemoved.length) && !isModerator) {\n      throw new ForbiddenError('Отметка посещения — только модератор');\n    }\n  }"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Ownership check that only covers booked_users and attended_users"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/collections/trainings/records/{id}",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "e7dc6b4eae0102bdc44783239c3637ec0cb2ee7dc18e6566d76ca71a606367ee"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "6.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "39d6a40305c0353c8c133cd1f6100ab9e41bdec8ea4852b54b112bf1f99b421e",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0003",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 6.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-12 15:19:49 UTC",
              "target": "https://app.milenkih-team.ru",
              "endpoint": "/api/collections/trainings/records/{id}",
              "method": "PATCH",
              "cwe": "CWE-863",
              "impact": "An authenticated ordinary club member can falsify any other member's training history on any training (past, present, or closed), making that member appear to have been removed from a training by a moderator. The forged badge \"Снят модератором\" and the training entry are rendered in that member's profile and in the moderator's member-profile view (`ProfileViewModal` / `ProfilePage`, via `getTrainingStatusForUser`). Because these fields are also consumed by booking eligibility and reporting logic, an attacker can inject spurious \"kicked\"/\"unbooked\" markers into other members' records at will. Every training in the instance is affected, and the write succeeds even on trainings where the attacker is not a participant. This is a cross-user integrity violation of records the business treats as authoritative attendance history.\n\nA second effect of the same root cause is that the attribution mechanism distinguishing a moderator removal from a system removal is also attacker-selectable: `restore_insufficient_users` drives `pb_hooks/trainings_audit.pb.js` to log a removal as a system action rather than a moderator action.\n\nA third effect of the same root cause is that the moderator-only guard on attendance marking can be switched off for a request by supplying `is_cancelled`. The authorization block containing the `attended_users` check is conditioned on transition flags derived from that caller-supplied value, and the exempted branch reaches `adjustAttendanceCountTx`, which writes the server-authoritative `users.attendance_count` with no authorization check. That counter feeds the achievement engine (`client/src/services/achievements.js`, `pb_hooks/achievementslib.js`, and the `profile.achievement.grant` audit path). The counter increment could not be persisted during this assessment because the only reachable training had already ended, which gates that adjustment off — a data-dependent proof gap rather than a control — so it is recorded as a control failure with the persisted effect unproven.",
              "technical_analysis": "The `trainings` collection update rule is `@request.auth.id != \"\"`, so any authenticated user may PATCH any training. Field-level authorization is delegated entirely to the JSVM hook `pb_hooks/trainings_booking_validate.pb.js`, which calls `trainingslib.assertTrainingUpdateAllowed` (field allow-list) and then `trainingslib.applyBookingSideEffects` (ownership check + side effects).\n\n`assertTrainingUpdateAllowed` treats five fields as user-editable for non-moderators (`TRAINING_USER_EDITABLE`, `pb_hooks/trainingslib.js:304`): `booked_users`, `unbooked_users`, `unpaid_booked_users`, `moderator_kicked_users`, `restore_insufficient_users`. It therefore permits the update to proceed.\n\n`applyBookingSideEffects` (`pb_hooks/trainingslib.js:394`) then enforces ownership **only** for four collections computed by `audit.newlyAdded` / `audit.newlyRemoved`:\n\n```javascript title=pb_hooks/trainingslib.js startLineNumber=394 endLineNumber=412\n  // --- IDOR (C-6): обычный пользователь — только себя; посещаемость — только модератор ---\n  if (!isCancelTransition && !isRestoreTransition) {\n    if (added.length && !isModerator) {\n      for (i = 0; i < added.length; i++) {\n        if (String(added[i]) !== authId) {\n          throw new ForbiddenError('Можно записать только себя');\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    if (removed.length && !isModerator) {\n      for (i = 0; i < removed.length; i++) {\n        if (String(removed[i]) !== authId) {\n          throw new ForbiddenError('Можно снять с записи только себя');\n        }\n      }\n    }\n    if ((attendedAdded.length || attendedRemoved.length) && !isModerator) {\n      throw new ForbiddenError('Отметка посещения — только модератор');\n    }\n  }\n```\n\n`added`/`removed` are derived solely from `booked_users`, and `attendedAdded`/`attendedRemoved` solely from `attended_users`. There is **no validation of `unbooked_users`, `moderator_kicked_users`, `unpaid_booked_users`, or `restore_insufficient_users`**. Writing a foreign user ID into `moderator_kicked_users` produces `added`/`removed`/`attendedAdded`/`attendedRemoved` that are all empty, so `hasWork` is false and the function returns without complaint — the record is saved with the attacker-controlled value.\n\n**Control-failure variant (same root cause).** The entire authorization block above is itself conditioned on two flags computed from the incoming record:\n\n```javascript title=pb_hooks/trainingslib.js startLineNumber=387 endLineNumber=395\n  var isCancelTransition = !original.getBool('is_cancelled') && record.getBool('is_cancelled');\n  var isRestoreTransition = original.getBool('is_cancelled') && !record.getBool('is_cancelled');\n  ...\n  // --- IDOR (C-6): обычный пользователь — только себя; посещаемость — только модератор ---\n  if (!isCancelTransition && !isRestoreTransition) {\n```\n\nBecause both flags derive from the caller-supplied `is_cancelled` value, a member can select the branch that skips the attendance check as well as the self-only checks. The exempted restore branch calls `adjustAttendanceCountTx(txApp, restoreAttended[i], 1)` (`:489-493`), which sets `users.attendance_count` and saves with no authorization. That write runs inside `$app.runInTransaction`, so it does not re-enter the `users` `onRecordUpdateRequest` hook and therefore never passes through the field-level ACL in `pb_hooks/users_access_guard_lib.js`. `assertTrainingUpdateAllowed` does not compensate, because `attended_users` is present in `TRAINING_COMPARE_FIELDS` but absent from `TRAINING_USER_EDITABLE`, so it is rejected only while that same transition conditional allows the block to run. Two independent controls are gated by one caller-controlled expression and fail together.\n\nGit history confirms the structure is long-standing: `git show 5ea0b5b:pb_hooks/trainingslib.js` (v0.39) already contains the same wrapper plus the same restore-branch call; the only later commit touching the hook (`efaa612`, v1.0.18) inserted `assertTrainingUpdateAllowed` in front of it without addressing the derived-flag precondition.\n\n**Downstream consequence of the forged fields.** `pb_hooks/trainings_audit.pb.js` uses `moderator_kicked_users` and `restore_insufficient_users` to interpret a removal: `addedInsufficient` populates `insufficientSet` and causes the event to be logged as a system action (\"Система сняла ... (недостаточно посещений)\"), while other foreign removals are attributed to a moderator (`booking.booking.cancel_moderator`).\n\nClient-side, `client/src/features/profile/ProfileViewModal.jsx:59` and `ProfilePage.jsx:53` compute the training status directly from `moderator_kicked_users`:\n\n```javascript title=client/src/features/profile/ProfileViewModal.jsx startLineNumber=59 endLineNumber=63\nfunction getTrainingStatusForUser(training, userId) {\n  const kicked = training.moderator_kicked_users || [];\n  if (kicked.includes(userId)) return 'kicked';\n  if (!hasTimeRangeEnded(training.date, training.duration || 0)) return 'booked';\n  return (training.attended_users || []).includes(userId) ? 'attended' : 'missed';\n}\n```\n\nwith `kicked` mapped to the badge label \"Снят модератором\". The attacker therefore controls how another member's attendance is presented.",
              "remediation_steps": "Extend the non-moderator ownership check in the trainings update hook so that every relation field the allow-list exposes to ordinary users is validated against the caller's own ID, not only `booked_users` and `attended_users`.\n\n1. In `applyBookingSideEffects`, additionally compute the added/removed sets for `unbooked_users`, `moderator_kicked_users`, `unpaid_booked_users`, and `restore_insufficient_users`, and require every added or removed identifier to equal the authenticated user's ID for non-moderators. Reject with the existing `ForbiddenError` otherwise.\n2. Alternatively, remove the moderator-semantic fields from the ordinary-user allow-list entirely. `moderator_kicked_users` and `restore_insufficient_users` represent moderator decisions and should not be writable by a plain user under any transition; ordinary users only need to add/remove themselves from `booked_users` (and their own `unbooked_users` entry that accompanies it).\n3. Make the authorization block independent of the cancel/restore transition. The flags `isCancelTransition` and `isRestoreTransition` are derived from the caller-supplied `is_cancelled` value and currently wrap the whole ownership and attendance check, so a caller can select the branch that skips them. Derive any transition handling from the persisted record, or apply the ownership and attendance checks unconditionally and treat the transition only as a side-effect selector.\n4. Treat `users.attendance_count` and the other membership counters as protected state. Updates performed with application privileges inside a transaction bypass the `users` field-level ACL, so any such write should assert the actor's authority explicitly at the point of the write rather than relying on the hook on the `users` collection.\n5. Add a server-side regression test that performs the foreign-ID PATCH described in the PoC and asserts a `403` for each of the four relation fields, including on trainings where the caller is not a participant, plus a case asserting that a non-moderator cannot change attendance through a cancel/restore-shaped request.\n\nBecause this issue is exploitable purely through direct API calls (bypassing the SPA), it cannot be mitigated with client-side changes.",
              "counterevidence": "The strongest case against this finding is that the affected fields might be entirely cosmetic and never influence a security decision. I tested that: `moderator_kicked_users` is read by `client/src/features/profile/ProfileViewModal.jsx` and `ProfilePage.jsx` to compute the displayed training status and badge, and by `client/src/features/admin/stats/StatsTrainingsCountModal.jsx` to expand training records; `restore_insufficient_users` is consumed by `pb_hooks/trainings_audit.pb.js` to attribute a removal to the system. So the values do drive both display and audit attribution. I also verified the write is not an artifact of a single training or of an unusual record state: it succeeded on all three trainings in the instance, including closed past ones, and with an explicit negative control (`booked_users` with the same ID returns 403). I confirmed that the values are durably stored by reading the record back after the write, and that referential integrity is enforced (invalid or malformed IDs are rejected with 400), so this is limited to writing real member IDs into these fields rather than injecting arbitrary strings. I did not observe any change to the victim's membership counters (`available_sessions`, `unpaid_sessions`, `used_sessions`, `attendance_count` all remained unchanged), so this is an integrity falsification of attendance records rather than a financial or session-quota impact — which is why it is rated a cross-user integrity issue rather than critical.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that club standings, membership billing, or tournament seeding are derived from these fields would raise the severity, as would a moderator-facing report that consumes `moderator_kicked_users` for capacity or eligibility decisions. Separately, a single end-to-end reproduction against a not-yet-ended training showing `users.attendance_count` incremented through the exempted restore branch would raise the severity by demonstrating a write to a server-authoritative statistic that drives achievement levels. Confirmation that the write is restricted to trainings the caller participates in would lower it.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "Setup: authenticate as an ordinary `user` (not moderator). Obtain a second, unrelated member's user ID from `GET /api/collections/users/records`.\n\n1. Confirm the legitimate cross-user booking guard rejects `booked_users` writes (negative control): send `PATCH /api/collections/trainings/records/{trainingId}` with body `{\"booked_users\":[\"<victimId>\"]}` and observe `403 {\"message\":\"Можно записать только себя.\"}`.\n2. Send the same PATCH with the victim's ID placed in `moderator_kicked_users` instead. The server returns `200` and persists the value.\n3. Send the same PATCH with the victim's ID in `unbooked_users`. The server returns `200` and persists the value.\n4. Read the record back with `GET /api/collections/trainings/records/{trainingId}` and observe the victim's ID stored in `moderator_kicked_users` and `unbooked_users`.\n5. Repeat step 2 against every other training record to show the write is not scoped to trainings the attacker participates in.\n6. Clean up by PATCHing the original values back.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-798",
          "ruleIndex": 3,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Production MAX bot token exposed in client-side environment file grants full bot API control and community IPI exfiltration\n\nThe production MAX messenger **bot token** used by the tennis mini-app is stored in `client/.env` as `MAX_BOT_TOKEN` and, because it is not `.gitignore`d, is committed to the Git repository. The token is **live**: it authenticates against `https://botapi.max.ru` as the real production bot `id420550689204_bot` (\"Тестовый бот\", user id `269693646`). Anyone with repository read access obtains full bot authority — reading the bot's chats and message history, sending messages as the bot to any user, and repointing the bot's webhook to a server they control, which then receives the raw MAX `user_id` of every member who starts the bot."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "client/.env"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 5,
                  "endLine": 5,
