            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "96d3abf7755993ed244a685c55710563d0035e5af6cfefcd374faaeb8095f9fb"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "3.1",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "9faae1a25c440dd88543a3309d840c7e13a28e2d1fb3fee10e9207c5184efc6c",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0006",
              "severity": "low",
              "cvss": 3.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-12 15:24:52 UTC",
              "target": "https://app.milenkih-team.ru",
              "cwe": "CWE-613",
              "impact": "A stolen token remains usable for about seven days with no way for the victim to revoke it, and refreshing does not cut off the old token.",
              "technical_analysis": "Tokens are stateless HS256 credentials tied to the record token key rather than server-side session state, so refresh adds a valid credential instead of replacing the old one. The client sign-out only clears the local auth store and never contacts the server. The administrative case is covered by the ban hook, which rotates the token key on ban.",
              "remediation_steps": "Add a server-side sign-out endpoint that rotates the record token key, call it from the client sign-out flow, and rotate the token key on each refresh so a refreshed session supersedes the previous credential.",
              "counterevidence": "No new attack surface on its own. Tokens are revoked on ban via token key rotation and banned records are rejected at the auth hooks, so only the voluntary sign-out path is missing. The token sits in origin-scoped localStorage, so a different user of the same profile does not inherit it.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Evidence that the token is additionally reachable by other scripts, such as a confirmed XSS, would raise this.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Capture a valid token. 2. Call auth-refresh and note the returned token differs. 3. Replay the original token and observe it is still accepted. 4. Attempt both plausible logout endpoints and observe 404.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-639",
          "ruleIndex": 6,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Broken object-level authorization on notifications collection allows any user to reassign someone else's notification (forged in-app notifications)\n\nThe PocketBase `notifications` collection accepts an UPDATE from any authenticated user that rewrites the `recipient` field to a different user's ID. A plain (non-moderator) account can therefore take a notification it owns, replace its text with arbitrary content, and re-address it to any other user — including moderators. Because the client lists notifications by `recipient = <my id>`, the forged record is displayed inside the victim's notification feed, impersonating official in-app messages."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "client/src/services/notifications.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 33,
                  "endLine": 37,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "export async function listNotifications(userId) {\n  return pb.collection('notifications').getFullList({\n    filter: pb.filter('recipient = {:uid}', { uid: userId }),\n    sort: '-created'\n  });\n}"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Victim-side read path: notifications are selected by recipient, so a reassigned record is delivered to the victim's feed"
              }
            },
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "client/src/services/notifications.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 123,
                  "endLine": 130,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "await pb.collection('notifications').create({\n      recipient: userId,\n      title: copy.title,\n      body: copy.body,\n      badge_text: copy.badge_text,\n      click_action: 'open_membership',\n      is_read: false\n    });"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Client-side creation with caller-supplied recipient: the primitive that lets an ordinary user seed a notification it can then re-address"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/collections/notifications/records/{id}",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "f0a30ed4bd6e557a891f6efa7b586e1e16e5ee5af6521d07af2e1ffa3f800c2d"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "3.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "36220d13ebc2267cf18acf1d053dd0e525c4dccc3e0d26d18cb050b722ccfe2e",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0007",
              "severity": "low",
              "cvss": 3.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-12 15:28:54 UTC",
              "target": "https://app.milenkih-team.ru (PocketBase API, `notifications` collection)",
              "endpoint": "/api/collections/notifications/records/{id}",
              "method": "PATCH",
              "cwe": "CWE-639",
              "impact": "An authenticated low-privilege user can inject arbitrary, attacker-controlled text into another user's in-app notification feed. Notifications are a trusted, branded channel in this application (membership expiry warnings, training reminders, broadcast messages) and the card renders the record's own `title`/`body` with a click action, so a forged card is indistinguishable from an official one. This supports targeted phishing/social engineering (e.g. \"оплатите абонемент по ссылке\", \"ваша запись отменена\") and can be aimed at moderators, who hold administrative functions. It also permits one-off corruption of a victim's notification state and, in combination with the notification deep-link handler, attacker-chosen navigation targets for social notifications (`meta.collection` / `meta.postId`). The write is unauthenticated-to-authorization-bypass: no moderator role and no ownership of the target user is required.",
              "technical_analysis": "PocketBase enforces authorization at the collection level through API rules. For `notifications`, the create rule binds `recipient` correctly (a plain user cannot create a record addressed to somebody else — every attempt returned HTTP 400), and the delete rule is ownership-scoped. The **update rule, however, does not pin `recipient` to the existing/authenticated owner**, so a single-field `PATCH` can move an existing record to another user id.\n\nThe write primitive is trivially reachable because the same collection granted the attacker create rights for records addressed to themselves. `client/src/services/notifications.js:123` (`maybeNotifySessionsLeft`) creates notifications client-side with a caller-supplied `recipient`, which is why a normal user account can seed a record it owns and then re-address it.\n\nObserved sequence against the live API with the account's own token:\n1. `POST /api/collections/notifications/records` with `{\"recipient\":\"<own id>\",\"title\":\"...\",\"body\":\"...\"}` → `200`, record created.\n2. `PATCH /api/collections/notifications/records/<id>` with `{\"recipient\":\"<other user id>\"}` → `200`, and the response body echoed `\"recipient\":\"<other user id>\"` together with the attacker-chosen `title`/`body`.\n3. Immediately afterwards the attacker's own `GET /api/collections/notifications/records/<id>` → `404`, while the reassignment response had returned the record milliseconds earlier — i.e. the record persisted with the new owner and left the attacker's read scope.\n\nControl experiments show this is collection-specific and not a blanket PocketBase misconfiguration:\n- `PATCH /api/collections/comments/records/<foreign id>` → `404` (comments update IS ownership-scoped).\n- `DELETE /api/collections/notifications/records/<foreign or nonexistent id>` → `404`, while deleting an owned record → `204` (delete IS ownership-scoped).\n- `POST` with a foreign `recipient` → `400` (create IS bound to the caller).\n\nRendering path: `src/features/notifications/NotificationCard.jsx:578-591` renders `notification.title` and `notification.body` through `PostContentHtml`, and `src/features/notifications/NotificationCard.jsx:596-604` renders `badge_text` and a click-action button. `PostContentHtml` sanitizes with an allowlist (`toDisplayHtml` → `sanitizePostHtml`), so the content lands as text/markup-limited HTML rather than script — this is a spoofing/authorization defect, not XSS. The victim's feed is populated by `src/services/notifications.js:33-37` (`listNotifications`, `filter: recipient = {:uid}`).",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Tighten the `notifications` update API rule so that a record can only be updated by the authenticated user it belongs to and so that the `recipient` field cannot change: for example require `recipient = @request.auth.id` on the update rule and keep the \"current recipient\" equality requirement, so that any update whose new `recipient` differs from the stored one is rejected.\n2. Serve the field value through a server-side guard as well as the rule: add an `onRecordUpdateRequest` hook on `notifications` that throws a `ForbiddenError` when `e.record.get('recipient') !== e.record.original().get('recipient')` for non-superuser callers, mirroring the existing `pb_hooks/users_access_guard.pb.js` pattern already used for the `users` collection.\n3. Restrict client-side creation of notifications. Privileged notification types (membership, training, broadcast) should be produced server-side by the existing hooks rather than by `client/src/services/notifications.js:123` (`maybeNotifySessionsLeft`), which lets any caller choose a `recipient`. If client-side creation must remain, bind `recipient` to `@request.auth.id` in the create rule.\n4. Treat notification `title`/`body`/`badge_text` as trusted branding: keep them immutable to non-superusers (consider excluding them from the update rule, or restricting updates to the `is_read` field) so the feed cannot be used as a phishing surface.\n5. Re-run authorization checks across the remaining collections with the same matrix (create/update/delete × own/foreign/nonexistent) — comments, gallery and tournament comments, posts, tournament_posts, trainings, products, achievements and the audit/log collections — since the notifications update rule shows the rules were written per-collection and at least one was left loose.",
              "counterevidence": "Actively tested the case against the finding. (1) Considered that the server may silently ignore the `recipient` change: refuted — the 200 response body itself returned `\"recipient\":\"z7piq9643k5995x\"`, and the subsequent 404 on read-back confirms the record moved out of the attacker's ownership scope (had the field been ignored, the record would still have been readable by the attacker). (2) Considered a server-side notification queue, approval flow or superuser-only write path: no such gate blocked the write; the same collection already grants the caller create rights for self-addressed records, so the write path is a normal member capability. (3) Considered a global PocketBase rule misconfiguration: refuted by the control tests — foreign comment PATCH is 404, foreign DELETE is 404, foreign-recipient POST is 400, i.e. only the notifications UPDATE rule is loose. (4) Considered XSS via the injected fields: `NotificationCard` renders `title`/`body` through `PostContentHtml`, which sanitizes to an allowlist, so the impact is spoofing rather than script execution and no XSS is claimed. (5) Considered whether the card is really shown to the victim: the list filter is `recipient = {:uid}`, so the forged record is in scope for the victim, but the attacker cannot read the victim's feed (404 on foreign ids); this last hop was not visually confirmed from the victim's session, which is the sole remaining proof gap and is why confidence on the *displayed* effect is stated as high-on-write / not verified-from-the-victim-side. (6) A deliberately attempted escalation — creating a notification for a foreign recipient directly — was rejected with 400, so the finding is limited to the update path.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "confidence_rationale": "The write primitive was executed live against the production API and verified three independent ways: the 200 response echoing the new `recipient`, the attacker's subsequent 404 on the same record, and the failing control tests on neighbouring collections and on the delete/create paths. The only element not directly observed is the rendered card inside the victim's own session (the attacker's token cannot read another user's notifications, by design); the client code path that selects and renders it (`listNotifications` filtered by `recipient`, `NotificationCard` rendering `title`/`body`) is confirmed from source.",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that the forged card is rendered inside the victim's notification feed from the victim's own session would leave this as a high-severity spoofing/authorization defect; evidence that a moderator also acts on the forged card's click action (e.g. a stored action that triggers a privileged server operation) would raise it to critical, while evidence that the notifications list is only ever populated server-side for the victim (making the reassigned record unreachable) would lower it to medium.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Authenticate as any ordinary (non-moderator) user and note your own user id and another user's id (user ids are enumerable by any authenticated user through `GET /api/collections/users/records`).\n2. Create a notification record addressed to yourself so you own it: `POST /api/collections/notifications/records` with `{\"recipient\":\"<your id>\",\"title\":\"...\",\"body\":\"...\",\"is_read\":false}`. Expect HTTP 200 and an `id` in the response.\n3. Replace the recipient with the victim's user id in a single-field update: `PATCH /api/collections/notifications/records/<id>` with body `{\"recipient\":\"<victim id>\"}`. Expect HTTP 200 and a response body whose `recipient` is now the victim while `title`/`body` remain attacker-controlled.\n4. Confirm the reassignment persisted by reading the record back with your own token: `GET /api/collections/notifications/records/<id>` returns HTTP 404 — the record has left your ownership scope.\n5. Log into the victim account (or observe its notification list) and confirm the forged card with the attacker-supplied title/body appears in the feed, since `listNotifications` queries `recipient = <victim id>`.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-602",
          "ruleIndex": 7,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Custom content-view route validation bypassed by direct collection write, allowing unbounded self-attributed metric rows\n\nThe application provides a dedicated endpoint, `POST /api/content-view`, that validates view-tracking input before storing it. The underlying `content_views` collection, however, remains directly writable through the generic PocketBase collection API with only a self-scoped rule, so an authenticated member can create view rows carrying `object_type` / `object_id` / `source` values that the application's own validator rejects. The rows cannot be read back or deleted by any non-superuser, so they accumulate permanently in the table that the moderator statistics dashboard aggregates."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "pb_hooks/statslib.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 856,
                  "endLine": 870,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "function createContentView(userId, payload) {\n  payload = payload || {};\n  var objectType = String(payload.object_type || payload.objectType || '');\n  var objectId = String(payload.object_id || payload.objectId || '');\n  var source = String(payload.source || '');\n\n  if (objectType !== 'post' && objectType !== 'tournament_post') {\n    return { error: 'object_type must be post or tournament_post', status: 400 };\n  }\n  if (!objectId) {\n    return { error: 'object_id required', status: 400 };\n  }\n  if (source && source !== 'viewport' && source !== 'modal') {\n    return { error: 'source must be viewport or modal', status: 400 };\n  }"
                  }
                }
              },
              "message": {
                "text": "Validation implemented on the application route but not enforced by the data store"
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/api/collections/content_views/records",
                  "kind": "endpoint"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "1975750ed076b8cc90f51fdf1dbceb0f0df6882109153e227edde6c7f3ff2d46"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "4.3",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "7b6696f6008881e27beba10f0d1a3bd93a1fcf0afb91560979ca82f5c1d77032",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0008",
              "severity": "medium",
              "cvss": 4.3,
              "timestamp": "2026-09-12 15:41:46 UTC",
              "target": "https://app.milenkih-team.ru",
              "endpoint": "/api/collections/content_views/records",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-602",
              "impact": "A member can write arbitrary, permanently-retained rows into the content-view metrics table. The application's validation — which restricts `object_type` to `post` or `tournament_post`, requires a non-empty `object_id`, and restricts `source` to `viewport` or `modal` — is bypassed. Because `content_views` has no delete rule for non-superusers, the rows cannot be cleaned up by their author, so the distortion is durable. The direct impact is capped: the statistics consumers are moderator-only and the reach report re-resolves each object server-side before presenting it, so a fabricated `object_id` does not inject a spurious entry into the displayed top-content list. The practical effect is inflated or meaningless view-metric data and unbounded table growth in an analytics collection.",
              "technical_analysis": "The application exposes a purpose-built write path for view tracking:\n\n```javascript title=pb_hooks/statslib.js startLineNumber=856 endLineNumber=878\nfunction createContentView(userId, payload) {\n  payload = payload || {};\n  var objectType = String(payload.object_type || payload.objectType || '');\n  var objectId = String(payload.object_id || payload.objectId || '');\n  var source = String(payload.source || '');\n\n  if (objectType !== 'post' && objectType !== 'tournament_post') {\n    return { error: 'object_type must be post or tournament_post', status: 400 };\n  }\n  if (!objectId) {\n    return { error: 'object_id required', status: 400 };\n  }\n  if (source && source !== 'viewport' && source !== 'modal') {\n    return { error: 'source must be viewport or modal', status: 400 };\n  }\n  ...\n```\n\nThe handler `POST /api/content-view` enforces these checks. However, the collection that the handler writes to remains directly reachable through PocketBase's standard record API, and its schema rule permits any authenticated user to create a row as long as the row is attributed to themselves:\n\n```text\ncontent_views.createRule = '@request.auth.id != \"\" && user = @request.auth.id'\ncontent_views.listRule  = null\ncontent_views.viewRule  = null\ncontent_views.updateRule = null\ncontent_views.deleteRule = null\n```\n\nBecause `listRule` and `deleteRule` are null, the collection is write-only for non-superusers: rows cannot be read back nor removed through the API. The validation implemented for the application's own route is therefore not a property of the data store, and the same input is accepted when submitted through the generic collection endpoint.\n\nThere is also no server-side de-duplication or per-object limit on either path, so row volume is bounded only by request count.\n\nThe consumption side does not compound the issue: `getReach` re-resolves each referenced object server-side via `$app.findRecordById` (`pb_hooks/statslib.js:302-313`) before presenting it, so a fabricated identifier cannot inject an arbitrary entry into the moderator report. All statistics endpoints additionally require the moderator role.",
              "remediation_steps": "Make the data store enforce the same constraints the application route enforces.\n\n1. Tighten the `content_views` collection rules so the generic record endpoint cannot be used as an alternate write path: restrict creation to server-side code (set the create rule to a value no client can satisfy and have the `POST /api/content-view` handler perform the write with application privileges), or add a collection-level `onRecordCreateRequest` hook that applies the same `object_type` / `object_id` / `source` validation as `createContentView`.\n2. Apply the validation in one place and reuse it: have the collection hook call the same logic used by the route, so the two paths cannot drift apart again.\n3. Add a delete rule (or a maintenance job) so that erroneous or abusive rows can be removed; currently no non-superuser can delete a row, and there is no visible retention policy for this table.\n4. Add rate limiting or server-side de-duplication (for example, one row per user/object/time-window) to bound how many rows a single account can create, on both the route and the collection endpoint.\n5. Purge the test rows left in the table: rows with `object_id` values prefixed `zqath3` were created during this assessment and could not be deleted through the API.",
              "counterevidence": "The strongest case against this finding is that it has no direct security consequence: every consumer of `content_views` is a moderator-only statistics endpoint, the reach report re-resolves each referenced object server-side via `$app.findRecordById`, so a fabricated `object_id` cannot inject a spurious row into the displayed report, and there is no cross-user read or write — the create rule forces `user = @request.auth.id` and any attempt to attribute a row to another user is rejected with `400`. I also verified that reading the table is denied to non-superusers (`403`), so a member cannot even confirm what was written. What remains is a genuine but low-impact control gap: validation implemented for the application's own route is not enforced by the data store, an alternate write path exists for the same table, and rows created through it are permanently unremovable because the delete rule is null. It is reported because it is a real inconsistency between two paths to the same data rather than as a high-impact issue.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that a player-facing or public surface derives a metric from `content_views` (for example popularity ordering, or content promoted on view count) would raise the severity, since the values would then be attacker-controlled and externally visible. Conversely, confirming that the table is retained only for internal aggregate statistics with no decision-making role would lower it to informational.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Authenticate as an ordinary `user` account.\n2. Send the malformed value through the application's own validated route to establish the negative control: `POST /api/content-view` with `object_type` set to an invalid value returns `400` with `object_type must be post or tournament_post`.\n3. Send the identical malformed value directly to the collection endpoint: `POST /api/collections/content_views/records` with the same invalid `object_type`. The server returns `200` and creates the record, demonstrating that the validator is bypassed.\n4. Confirm the row is durably stored and that the author cannot remove it: a subsequent `GET` on the record returns `403 Only superusers can perform this action`, and `DELETE` on the row also returns `403`.\n5. Repeat step 3 to show that nothing limits the number of rows that can be created.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/missing-authentication-on-custom-route-bfla-api5",
