        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/stored-xss-phishing-via-attacker-controlled-notification-title-body-cross-user-notification-forgery",
          "ruleIndex": 40,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Stored XSS / phishing via attacker-controlled notification title/body; cross-user notification forgery — Ruled out: POST /api/collections/notifications/records\n\ncreateRule = `@request.auth.id != \"\" && (recipient = @request.auth.id || @request.auth.role = \"moderator\")`. Live: POST with recipient=<self> and attacker-chosen title/body/badge_text/click_action/meta/training_id/training_state -> 200 (persisted, listed back). POST with recipient=<other user id> -> 400 \"Failed to create record.\" (rule holds). XSS neutralised: NotificationCard.jsx:581-590 renders title and body through PostContentHtml -> toDisplayHtml -> sanitizePostHtml, an allow-list sanitiser (postRichText.js:13-36, 224-230, 269-395) that whitelists only B/STRONG/I/EM/U/A/BR/DIV/P/SPAN/IMG, strips every event handler and `style`-carrying attribute except an explicit set, and restricts href to ^https?:// or ^mailto: (lines 348-353); `javascript:` is dropped, so the <img src=x onerror=...> payload is inert. The only dangerouslySetInnerHTML in the codebase is PostContentHtml.jsx:108/120 which is always fed the sanitised output. Rows are self-scoped by rule, so any residual rendering issue is self-XSS. 3 marker rows (prefix zqath3) were created and all were deleted (2x204, 1x404 after the auto-created one was already removed); the list now holds only the two legitimate pre-existing rows."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "POST /api/collections/notifications/records"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "strix": {
              "coverage_outcome": "ruled_out",
              "risk_area": "Stored XSS / phishing via attacker-controlled notification title/body; cross-user notification forgery",
              "surface": "POST /api/collections/notifications/records",
              "recorded_by": "Achievements & Score Forgery Hunter",
              "source": "agent_reported"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/stored-xss-via-self-editable-unvalidated-avatar-url",
          "ruleIndex": 41,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Stored XSS via self-editable unvalidated avatar_url — No issue identified: users.avatar_url (self-editable field → &lt;img src&gt; sink)\n\nusers.updateRule allows id = @request.auth.id, and avatar_url is in SELF_EDITABLE_ALWAYS (users_access_guard_lib.js:8) with no URL-scheme validation. Live: PATCH {\"avatar_url\":\"javascript:alert(1)\"} -> 200 and the value persisted; restored to the original MAX CDN URL afterwards. Sink verified in source: client/src/lib/avatar.js:66-67 returns {hasAvatar:true, src:user.avatar_url} verbatim and client/src/components/ui/Avatar.jsx:21 interpolates it into <img src=...>. Recorded as an observation rather than a report: React blocks `javascript:` URLs for img src, so this does not give script execution; the residual impact is an off-origin image load (viewer IP/UA + referrer leakage to an attacker host) and content/professional spoofing, and the same field is already legitimately populated with an external URL by the MAX Bridge auth path (pb_hooks/max-auth.pb.js:114), so the field is external by design. Low-value hardening: allow-list the scheme."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "users.avatar_url (self-editable field → &lt;img src&gt; sink)"
                }
